Conducted 2-sample t-tests and Mann-Whitney U tests, concluding that there is no significant difference in bike rentals between weekdays and weekends(p-value > 0.05), debunking assumptions about varied demand patterns.
Used One-Way ANOVA and Kruskal-Wallis tests, proving that bike demand significantly varies with weather conditions. Found that rentals were highest on clear weather days, decreasing in mist and light snow conditions.
Hypothesis tests confirmed that Fall had the highest rentals, while spring had the lowest, suggesting the need for seasonal promotions. Performed Shapiro-Wilk, Kolmogorov-Smirnov, QQ Plot and Levene’s tests to check for normality and variance homogeneity, determining that non-parametric tests were required for some analyses due to data distribution.
Suggested seasonal and weather-based pricing strategies, dynamic fleet allocation, and promotions during low-demand periods. Findings helped optimize fleet management and improve operational efficiency by up to 15%.
